course: dictionary
lesson: 4644
title: Histamine and allergies
level: primary-6
stars: 0
explain: Histamine is a chemical your body makes. When pollen, dust or an insect bite gets in, your body can react as if it is an enemy and release histamine. «Hay fever is an allergy to pollen», and histamine is what causes the sneezing, itching and runny nose.
explain: Think of your immune system as your body's defence team. In an allergy it makes a mistake: «An allergy is your immune system making a mistake: it treats something harmless, like pollen or peanuts, as an enemy and releases histamine.»
explain: Histamine is not a germ. Germs such as bacteria, viruses and fungi cause infections, but histamine is a substance the body releases during an allergy. Hives are raised, itchy red patches caused by histamine.
explain: Medicines called antihistamines block histamine and can help with hay fever, rashes and itchy bites. Only take medicine given by an adult. Serious allergies need an EpiPen and an ambulance.
fact: Histamine is a chemical made by your body.
fact: Histamine causes allergy symptoms such as sneezing, itching, swelling and a runny nose.
fact: «Hives» are raised, itchy red patches caused by histamine.
fact: Hay fever is worse on dry, windy days, when more pollen is blown about.
fact: Histamine is not a germ.
fact: Only take medicine given by an adult; serious allergies need an EpiPen and an ambulance.
exercise: What is histamine? | A germ that makes you ill | A type of pollen blown by the wind | A sweet you eat when you have a cold | *A chemical the body makes that causes allergy symptoms
exercise: «Hay fever is an allergy to pollen.» Which of these makes your body release histamine in hay fever? | Rain | *Pollen | Sweets | Sunshine
exercise: «Germs include bacteria, viruses and fungi; histamine is not a germ.» Which of these is a germ? | Peanuts | Histamine | Pollen | *Bacteria
test: «Hives» are raised, itchy red patches. What causes them? | Dust settling on the floor | *Histamine | Sunshine on the skin | Germs only
test: Which kind of medicine blocks histamine? | Ice cream | Vitamin water | Pollen | *Antihistamine
exam: Someone has swelling of the lips or tongue and trouble breathing. What should you do? | Wait until the next day to see if it gets better | Give them a sweet and wait | *Get an adult and call for an ambulance straight away | Give them an antihistamine from the cupboard
